ces at only 21-27%"], "rationale": "Abbott's incumbency, massive financial edge, and Texas's Republican lean keep him favored, but September polling has narrowed the race to a few points and several surveys show Hinojosa tied or ahead, making this a competitive Lean Republican race rather than a comfortable one.", "watch": "If Hinojosa's late-September polling strength holds through October while Abbott's crime-focused attacks fail to move the numbers, this could shift to a genuine tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Greg Abbott (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Governor Greg Abbott seeks another term against state Representative Gina Hinojosa, with Libertarian Pat Dixon also on the ballot. Texas has leaned Republican by about six points in recent partisan margins, and Abbott enters with a dominant financial position, having raised roughly $25 million and holding about $67 million on hand compared with Hinojosa's $3 million.\n\nStill, the race has tightened. Polling through late September averages a Republican lead of only two to three points, with several surveys showing a dead heat or a narrow Hinojosa edge. Most race raters call it Lean Republican, though two rate it Likely Republican, and prediction markets give Hinojosa roughly a one-in-four chance. Abbott's crime-focused attacks and his advertising war chest remain significant obstacles for a Democrat trying to win statewide.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1819, "margin": -4.27}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2694, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2894, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2702, "margin": -3.5}]}